Milo was a small gray cat with one white paw and a habit of watching the world from the windowsill. He lived in a quiet house at the end of Maple Lane, where the days were soft and predictable: breakfast at seven, naps in the afternoon, and dinner when the kitchen light flickered on.

But Milo dreamed of more.

He dreamed of the forest beyond the garden, where the wind smelled of pine and rain, and where birds called to one another in voices he could never quite understand.

One morning, the gate to the garden was left open.

Milo sat at the threshold, tail flicking. The world beyond looked enormous. He took one cautious step, then another, until the familiar scent of home faded behind him and the wild air filled his whiskers.

The forest was louder than he expected. Leaves crackled under his paws. A squirrel chattered from above, and a stream whispered over smooth stones. Milo followed the sound of water until he reached a clearing where sunlight fell in golden patches.

There, he met a crow with glossy black feathers and a curious eye.

The crow tilted its head. “You’re far from home,” it seemed to say.

Milo blinked. “I know,” he replied, though he had no words—only the quiet certainty in his heart.

The crow flew ahead, and Milo followed. They crossed a fallen log, climbed a mossy hill, and slipped beneath a curtain of ivy. Along the way, Milo discovered things he had never known: the taste of wild mint, the cool shade beneath ferns, the thrill of chasing a leaf in the wind.

For the first time, he felt brave.

But as the sun began to dip low, the forest grew darker. The sounds changed. The birds grew quiet. A distant howl rolled through the trees, and Milo’s ears flattened.

He missed the windowsill. He missed the warm bowl by the kitchen door. He missed the sound of his human’s voice calling his name.

The crow landed beside him and gave a soft caw, as if to say, *It’s time to go home.*

Milo turned and ran.

He raced through the forest, leaping over roots and ducking under branches. The path seemed longer now, but he did not stop. When he finally burst through the gate and into the garden, the kitchen light was glowing.

His owner stood at the door, worried and relieved.

“There you are, little adventurer,” she said softly, lifting him into her arms.

Milo purred, pressing his head against her hand. He had seen the world beyond the garden, and he knew it was vast and wild and wonderful.

But home, he decided, was the best place of all.
